From ff993f01ddf8064d94d8b002578a596310ebb427 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Pawelczyk <havner@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 20:32:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] wayland/dma-buf: Give up the scanout tranche for
 clients that can't use it

Some clients keep committing buffers the primary plane can't scan out even
after being offered the scanout tranche, and re-offering it doesn't help.
Indiana Jones and the Great Circle via Wine on NVIDIA is one: its swapchain
images use a compressed modifier the plane rejects, and every time the
scanout tranche arrives its GPU work stalls for 2 s, the game recreates its
swapchain (again with the compressed modifier) and the cycle repeats,
giving ~0.5 fps in fullscreen while compositing runs at full rate.

Record when the scanout tranche was first offered to a surface since its
last successful scanout. If scanout still fails because the buffer's
format/modifier isn't supported by the primary plane more than a second
after that, stop offering the tranche to the surface. Well-behaved clients
switch to a scanout-capable buffer within a few frames (the Vulkan WSI
reports VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR on the next acquire) and are unaffected.
The surface stays a fullscreen (VRR) candidate.

Once given up, don't keep attempting scanout on every frame while the
surface's buffer can't go on the primary plane: reject it as a scanout
candidate up front. A buffer that happens to be scanout-capable is still
tried.

Also only re-send a surface's dma-buf feedback when its tranches actually
changed. The scanout candidate flips on every buffer detach/attach, e.g.
while Wine recreates a swapchain, and NVIDIA's Vulkan WSI treats any
feedback event as a reason to mark the swapchain out of date. With the
tranche given up, the identical re-sends kept the game recreating its
swapchain over a dozen times a second.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
